          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure, or hypertension, is a condition where the force of the blood against the artery walls is consistently too high. It is usually classified based on repeated measurements rather than a single high reading. When diagnosing hypertension, healthcare providers typically look for blood pressure readings that are high on multiple occasions.\n\nA single high reading can be caused by many factors, such as stress, activity level, or even the environment. Therefore, a diagnosis of hypertension usually requires at least two or more elevated readings taken at different times to confirm that it is a persistent condition.\n\nI could not provide specific guidelines for diagnosis or detailed management strategies, as that information was not available in the sources I accessed.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
